Slide 1: What are Variables? • Variables are containers that store information • They have a name (label) and a value (data) • Used to remember and use data in programs • Example: age = 20 (age = container, 20 = value)



Slide 2: How to Store Data • Write variable_name = value • The value can be a number, text, or True/False • Computer keeps the latest value if updated • Variables make programs dynamic and smart



Slide 3: Data Types – int and float • int → Whole numbers (example: 10, 2025) • Used for counting or math • float → Decimal numbers (example: 3.14, 5.8) • Used for precision values like height or price



Slide 4: Data Types – str and bool • str → Text inside quotes (example: "Hello") • Used for names, messages, etc. • bool → True or False • Used for decisions and conditions



Slide 5: Checking Type & Changing Values • Use type(variable) to check data type • Variables can change anytime • score = 10 → score = 20 → score = score + 5 • Always latest value is stored



Slide 6: Good Name Rules + Recap • No spaces in names (use _ instead) • Do not start names with numbers • Use meaningful names • Recap: Variables store data → int, float, str, bool → type() checks type → values can change → follow naming rules



L3 
Slide 1: Input & Output in Python
• print() → Python speaks to you
• input() → Python listens to you
• Used for communication between user and program
• Simple and most important part of Python for beginners

Slide 2: Output using print()
• print() shows messages or values on screen
• Can display text, numbers, variables
• Example: print("Hello!")
• Python speaks to the user with print()

Slide 3: Input using input()
• input() takes typing from the user
• Always gives result as a string (text)
• Example: name = input("Enter your name: ")
• Python listens to the user with input()

Slide 4: Converting Input to Numbers
• input() returns string → convert when needed
• Convert to integer → int()
• Convert to decimal → float()
• Example: age = int(input("Enter your age: "))

Slide 5: Make Your Print Beautiful + Mini Example
• Use f-strings for clean output
• Example: print(f"My name is {name}")
• Mini program idea: ask name, age, city → show profile
• Practice combining input + print every day



L4
Slide 1: Arithmetic Operators
• Used for math in Python
• + Add
• – Subtract
• * Multiply
• / Divide
• // Floor divide (no decimals)
• % Remainder
• ** Power
• Example: 10 % 3 → 1

Slide 2: Comparison Operators
• Used to check values
• Always give True or False
• == Equal
• != Not equal
• > Greater than
• < Less than
• >= Greater or equal
• <= Less or equal
• Example: age >= 18 → True or False

Slide 3: Logical Operators
• Combine multiple conditions
• and → Both must be True
• or → Any one True is enough
• not → Reverse True/False
• Helps Python make decisions
• Example: age > 18 and age < 60

Slide 4: Assignment Operators
• Store and update values
• = assign value
• x += 3 means x = x + 3
• x -= 2 means x = x - 2
• x *= 4 means x = x * 4
• x /= 5 means x = x / 5
• Used to keep changing data

Slide 5: Using Operators for Decisions
• Conditions control the program
• if something is True → do this
• else → do something else
• Uses comparison + logical operators
• Example idea: check age to vote
• Example idea: check even or odd

Slide 6: Real Mini Examples
• Check voting
If age >= 18 → print “You can vote”
• Even number check
If number % 2 == 0 → “Even”
• Salary increase
salary += 5000 → updated salary
• Adult check
age > 18 and age < 60 → “Adult”

